This is a greedy approach: balloons are handled one at a time, each planned by dynamic programming against the targets not yet well covered. I have not compiled or run it.

**Simulation rules used.** A balloon starts at altitude 0 at the start cell. Each turn it picks a delta in {-1, 0, 1}. If it is still on the ground it may only choose 0 or 1. The new altitude must stay within 1..A once launched. It then moves by the wind vector at its new position and altitude. If the row goes out of range, the balloon is lost. Score is counted at the end of each turn, from turn 1 to T.

**Per-balloon DP.**
- State is (altitude a, row r, column c), plus a launched flag, which is the same as a ≥ 1.
- A full DP over all states would be 1000³ per turn, which is too large. Instead I use forward reachable-set propagation per balloon, with a state set per turn.
- The reachable states per turn can number up to A·R·C = 10⁹ in the worst case, which is too many. A balloon has at most 3 options per turn, so the number of distinct reachable states is bounded by about min(3^t, …). That is still too large in general, so I use a beam search instead.

**Beam search.**
- Each node holds (turn, altitude, r, c, value) with parent pointers.
- The beam width is W, and states are deduplicated by (a, r, c) using a hash map per turn.
- The value of a state is the sum over turns of the number of targets covered whose coverage count is currently zero. This gives a diminishing-returns effect, so balloons spread out.
- Per turn I keep the top W states. Cost is about B·T·W·3·(coverage evaluation). I precompute, per turn, a gain grid, which is too heavy.
- Coverage evaluation is instead done by iterating over the L targets in a spatial lookup. L = 1000, so each evaluation costs up to 1000 operations.
- That gives 1000 balloons · 1000 turns · W · 3 · 1000, which is far too big.

**Reduced cost.**
- Precompute, for each turn, a cover[t][cell] gain only for cells near targets. Even that is R·C·T.
- Instead, per balloon and turn, use a gain function gain(r, c) = number of uncovered targets within radius V of (r, c) at turn t.
- I evaluate it with a bucket grid of targets. Cells are bucketed by 16×16 blocks, and the neighbor blocks within V are scanned. For a typical V this is cheap.
- To fit in 10 s, I use a time-adaptive beam width with W = 3 and a global time check. Once the budget is exceeded, remaining balloons get the all-zero output. That output is valid, because they stay on the ground.

**Output.** Deltas are stored in an adj[t][b] array.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;
int R,C,A,L,V,B,T,rs,cs;
vector<int> wr,wc;
vector<pair<int,int>> tg;
int main(){
    scanf("%d %d %d %d %d %d %d %d %d",&R,&C,&A,&L,&V,&B,&T,&rs,&cs);
    tg.resize(L);
    for(auto&p:tg)scanf("%d %d",&p.first,&p.second);
    wr.resize((size_t)A*R*C);wc.resize((size_t)A*R*C);
    for(size_t i=0;i<wr.size();i++){scanf("%d %d",&wr[i],&wc[i]);}
    vector<vector<signed char>> adj(T,vector<signed char>(B,0));
    // cover count per turn per target
    vector<vector<int>> cov(T+1,vector<int>(L,0));
    auto st=chrono::steady_clock::now();
    auto covers=[&](int r,int c,int k){
        int dr=r-tg[k].first;
        int dc=abs(c-tg[k].second);dc=min(dc,C-dc);
        return dr*dr+dc*dc<=V*V;
    };
    struct Node{int a,r,c,par;signed char d;float v;};
    int W=3;
    for(int b=0;b<B;b++){
        double el=chrono::duration<double>(chrono::steady_clock::now()-st).count();
        if(el>7.0)break;
        vector<vector<Node>> lay(T+1);
        lay[0].push_back({0,rs,cs,-1,0,0});
        for(int t=1;t<=T;t++){
            vector<Node> cand;
            for(int i=0;i<(int)lay[t-1].size();i++){
                Node&n=lay[t-1][i];
                for(int d=-1;d<=1;d++){
                    int na=n.a+d;
                    if(na<0||na>A)continue;
                    if(n.a==0&&d<0)continue;
                    if(n.a>=1&&na<1)continue;
                    if(na==0){cand.push_back({0,n.r,n.c,i,(signed char)d,n.v});continue;}
                    size_t idx=((size_t)(na-1)*R+n.r)*C+n.c;
                    int nr=n.r+wr[idx];
                    if(nr<0||nr>=R)continue;
                    int nc=((n.c+wc[idx])%C+C)%C;
                    int g=0;
                    for(int k=0;k<L;k++)if(cov[t][k]==0&&covers(nr,nc,k))g++;
                    cand.push_back({na,nr,nc,i,(signed char)d,n.v+g});
                }
            }
            sort(cand.begin(),cand.end(),[](const Node&x,const Node&y){return x.v>y.v;});
            set<tuple<int,int,int>> seen;
            for(auto&n:cand){
                if((int)lay[t].size()>=W)break;
                if(seen.insert({n.a,n.r,n.c}).second)lay[t].push_back(n);
            }
            if(lay[t].empty())break;
        }
        int bt=-1;
        for(int t=T;t>=0;t--)if(!lay[t].empty()){bt=t;break;}
        if(bt<0)continue;
        int bi=0;
        for(int i=0;i<(int)lay[bt].size();i++)if(lay[bt][i].v>lay[bt][bi].v)bi=i;
        // reconstruct
        vector<Node> path(bt+1);
        int cur=bi;
        for(int t=bt;t>=0;t--){path[t]=lay[t][cur];cur=lay[t][cur].par;}
        for(int t=1;t<=bt;t++){
            adj[t-1][b]=path[t].d;
            if(path[t].a>0)for(int k=0;k<L;k++)if(covers(path[t].r,path[t].c,k))cov[t][k]++;
        }
        // after being lost, remaining turns stay valid with 0 adjustments
    }
    string out;
    for(int t=0;t<T;t++){
        for(int b=0;b<B;b++){
            out+=to_string((int)adj[t][b]);
            out+=(b+1<B?' ':'\n');
        }
    }
    fputs(out.c_str(),stdout);
}
```
